+
+For compressible 1 MiB DTO-like payloads, the shaped link moves from about 3 QPS raw to 124 QPS while saving about 99.98% of business bytes. For the mixed 1 MiB pattern it moves from about 3 to 11.4 QPS while saving about 74.3%. For random data, candidate rejection is 100%, wire savings are zero, and throughput remains essentially raw; the remaining CPU/allocation cost is the attempted candidate compression, which is exactly why per-payload savings policy is retained in Core.
+
+## Disabled-compression fast-path A/B
+
+Same-runner A/B evidence run: https://github.com/SunSi12138/SharpLink/actions/runs/34025165413
+
+This compares the PR Stage-1 head `5a025579` against the final Stage-2 code candidate with **no providers registered on either side**. Both revisions run on the same GitHub-hosted runner, use the common pre-Stage-2 `EchoAsync(string)` RPC, and are interleaved Stage1 -> Stage2 -> Stage2 -> Stage1 to reduce temporal bias.
+
+Matrix:
+
+- TCP + SharedMemory;
+- 4 KiB / 64 KiB / 256 KiB / 1 MiB string payloads;
+- requested concurrency 1 / 8 / 32 / 128, using the same 75%-of-8-MiB effective-concurrency cap described above;
+- two rounds per revision;
+- QPS, P50/P99/P99.9, CPU/op, B/op, GC.
+
+Across the 32 transport/payload/concurrency scenarios, the median Stage2/Stage1 ratios are:
+
+| Metric | Median ratio | Interpretation |
+|---|---:|---|
+| QPS | 1.0593 | no systematic throughput regression |
+| P99 | 0.9749 | no systematic tail-latency regression; lower is better |
+| CPU us/op | 0.9442 | no systematic CPU regression |
+| allocated B/op | 1.00002 | allocation is effectively unchanged |
+
+These ratios are regression evidence for the empty-provider fast path, not a claim that Stage 2 universally improves performance. Individual short benchmark rows vary in both directions, as expected on shared hosted hardware.
+
+## Complexity and ownership result
+
+The Zstd work does not reverse the Stage-1 Core simplification. `SharpLink.Runtime` still contains no Brotli, Zstd, CRC, compressor factory, or algorithm-specific parser. Stage 2 adds a separate optional package that implements the public SPI and owns its own standard wire profile.
+
+The final ownership split remains:
+
+```text
+SharpLink.Runtime
+  negotiation / session binding / adaptive savings policy
+  generic originalLength framing and resource accounting
+  call/stream failure isolation
+
+SharpLink.Compression.Zstd
+  standard Zstd frame profile
+  checksum / dictionary / trailing / window semantics
+  backend implementation and backend-specific validation
+```
+
+The generic Core patch for Stage 2 is limited to clarifying zero-byte representation semantics and removing the accidental one-byte inbound assumption. All Zstd format machinery stays outside Core.
+
+## Final decision
+
+**Go.**
+
+- The Core boundary is materially smaller than the old built-in Brotli + `SCP1 + CRC32` design and does not need privileged algorithm hooks.
+- The public SPI can host an official provider while keeping integrity and frame semantics in the provider/profile.
+- The .NET 10 backend meets the tested correctness, bounds, cancellation, concurrency, packaging, and NativeAOT requirements on the validated platform matrix.
+- The fixed standard profile interoperates in both directions with the .NET 11 Preview BCL backend on Linux, Windows, and macOS, so a later stable-backend switch does not require a new profile if GA revalidation remains green.
+- WAN-like evidence shows material value for compressible/mixed large payloads, while random data is rejected back to raw.
+- SharedMemory/local evidence shows why the feature must remain optional/adaptive rather than part of codec identity.
+- Same-runner A/B evidence does not show a systematic regression when compression is disabled.
+
+The shipping choice is therefore `SharpLink.Compression.Zstd` on `net10.0` with `ZstdSharp.Port` 0.8.8, plus a future stable .NET 11 backend switch only after GA revalidation under the same `zstd-rfc8878-w23-checksum/v1` profile.
